<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antaeus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antaeus was a giant of Greek mythology, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and Gaia, who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and forced travellers to wrestle with him, killing them. He drew his strength from contact with the earth, his mother, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> defeated him by lifting him off the ground and crushing him. Ancient writers placed his home and tomb in Mauretania, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> (Tangier) or Lixus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antaeus.jpg" alt="Antaeus" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herakles_Antaios_Louvre_G103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
